5.6.3 更强的具身基座模型带来更好的 VLA
我们接下来研究,具身基础模型能否为 VLA 后训练提供更强的初始化。RynnBrain‐VLA 与 Qwen‐Based‐VLA 遵循相同的 VLA 后训练方案,并使用相同的动作块长度,但分别从 RynnBrain 和 Qwen Based Model 初始化。如表 6 所示,RynnBrain‐VLA 在全部三个长时程任务上均持续优于 Qwen‐Based‐VLA。其平均过程得分从 68.33% 提升至 91.28%,平均最终成功率从 60.00% 提升至 86.67%。这一优势在 Grab the Spatulas 上尤为明显:RynnBrain‐VLA 达到 95% 的成功率,而 Qwen‐Based‐VLA 为 50%。这些结果表明,在具身基础模型训练期间获得的能力能够有效迁移到下游 VLA 后训练中。与直接将通用 VLM 后训练为 VLA 策略相比,从 RynnBrain 初始化可使多阶段操作任务 执行更可靠,并显著减少中间阶段的失败。这一对比提供了直接证据,表明更强的具身基座模型可以作为真 实世界 VLA 策略更好的基础。
5.6.4 跨具身泛化
为了检验 RynnBrain‐VLA 的能力是否与特定的机器人具身绑定,我们通过第 4.3 节所述的部署框架将其适 配到三个在形态和机械结构上显著不同的机器人平台。如表 6 和图 8 所示,RynnBrain‐VLA 在人形机器人、 灵巧手操作、全身控制以及双臂抓取场景中均取得了稳定性能。这些具身在形态、动作维度和底层控制接口 方面存在显著差异。上述场景下的一致性能表明,RynnBrain 学习到的具身表征捕捉到了任务层面和交互 层面的知识,而这些知识并未与特定的机器人形态或动作参数化紧密耦合,从而使同一个具身基础模型能够 作为异构机器人策略的共享起点。
更有趣的是,我们发现,在单个 VLA 中联合训练多个任务和多种具身本体并不会引入明显的干扰。采用第 4.2 节中的统一动作空间策略,与分别微调的单任务策略相比,RynnBrain‐ VLAGeneralist 将平均过程得分 从 91.28% 提升至 94.14%,并将最终成功率从 86.67% 提升至 91.67%。我们假设,不同的具身本体共享共 同的交互结构——例如物体可供性、任务进展以及伸手—抓取—操作模式——尽管它们可执行的动作不同。因 此,联合训练使这些共享结构能够强化共同的视觉—动作表征,同时统一动作空间


preserves embodiment-specific control. This suggests that cross-embodiment data can act as complementary supervision: the key benefit of a unified VLA may lie not only in supporting more robots with one model, but also in allowing different embodiments to teach each other how the physical world can be interacted with.
6Conclusion and Future Work
We presentedRynnBrain 1.1, an embodied foundation model family spanning 2B, 9B, and 122B-A10B scales. RynnBrain 1.1 studies embodied capability scaling under a unified training framework and demonstrates clear improvements in multi-view reasoning, spatial grounding, and robot-oriented understanding. We further introduce explicit 3D-grounded supervision for the 2B and 9B models, enabling native language-conditioned 3D grounding, together with a compact contact-centered representation for interaction grounding.
We also develop RynnBrain-VLA to connect embodied understanding with real-world action. Under the same post-training recipe, RynnBrain-VLA substantially outperforms a Qwen-based VLA on long-horizon manipulation tasks. Through a unified action space with embodiment-specific masking, the policy is deployed across a Unitree G1 humanoid, an Astribot bimanual robot, and a Tianji-Wuji dexterous-hand system. Joint multi-task and multi-embodiment training further improves real-robot performance, providing initial evidence that heterogeneous robot data can contribute complementary supervision to a shared policy.
Looking forward, we aim to extend RynnBrain toward a unified multimodal model that combines perception, understanding, generation, spatial grounding, and action within a common framework. An important direction is to develop RynnBrain into the cognitive core of a general embodied agent, integrating long-term memory, world modeling, task planning, active perception, and closed-loop interaction. Such an agent could continuously reason about changes in the physical world, generate and revise plans from feedback, and acquire new skills through interaction across diverse tasks and robot embodiments.
